.TH 9P 2
.SH NAME
Srv, Req, serve, hold, held, respond, respond_all, answer_reads \- a server whose reads park
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:lib9p"
.PP
.nf
.B "Srv :: struct { fd: int, handler: vectra9.Handler, state: rawptr, msize: int, keep_on_remove: bool, ... }"
.B "Req :: struct { srv: ^Srv, tag: vectra9.Tag, msg: vectra9.Msg, frame: []u8, payload: []u8, held: bool, ... }"
.B "serve :: proc(srv: ^Srv) -> (served: u64, why: libuser.Serve_End)"
.B "hold :: proc(srv: ^Srv)"
.PP
.B "Wants :: #type proc(arg: rawptr, request: ^vectra9.Msg) -> bool"
.B "Drain :: #type proc(arg: rawptr, buf: []u8) -> int"
.B "held :: proc(srv: ^Srv, arg: rawptr, wants: Wants) -> (req: ^Req, ok: bool)"
.B "answer_reads :: proc(srv: ^Srv, arg: rawptr, wants: Wants, drain: Drain)"
.B "respond :: proc(req: ^Req, reply: vectra9.Msg) -> bool"
.B "respond_all :: proc(srv: ^Srv, reply: vectra9.Msg)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lib9p
is a 9P server on
.IR sys/libthread ,
answered from whichever thread of the server's own proc has the answer,
rather than one request at a time on the caller's stack the way
.IR serve (2)
is.
A caller fills in
.IR fd ,
.I handler
and
.I msize
in a
.B Srv
and calls
.IR serve .
The handler is a
.BR vectra9.Handler ,
the same signature
.IR serve (2)
takes: a server moves its
.I hold
and
.I respond
calls in and out of this library without changing anything else about
it.
.PP
.I Serve
reads a frame through an io proc, so the thread that runs it parks and
the proc does not, decodes it into a
.B Req
taken from the heap, and calls the handler.
A handler that can answer at once leaves its reply in place; one that
cannot calls
.I hold
before it returns, and
.I serve
keeps the request on a list rather than answering it, then reads the
next frame.
Nothing here is locked: the handler, the held list and the reply buffer
all belong to one proc, and a thread of that proc runs until it blocks.
.PP
.I Held
finds the oldest held request for which
.I wants
answers
.BR true ,
so a keyboard thread can answer the read of the window a key was typed
into and not another's.
.I Answer_reads
calls it in a loop: for each held request
.I wants
accepts, it takes what
.I drain
has to give and calls
.I respond
with an
.BR Rread ,
stopping when a drain answers nothing or no held request is left.
Every device-backed server in this tree drives its reads this way.
.PP
.I Respond
answers a request and frees its record, from any thread of the server's
proc: the loop itself, for a request the handler answered outright, or
whichever thread later has the answer to one that was held.
.I Respond_all
answers every still-held request with
.IR reply ,
which a server calls as it stops, so no client is left waiting on a
record nobody will ever fill.
.PP
.B Tflush
never reaches the handler.
A flush that names a held request drops it, unanswered, and
.I serve
answers
.B Rflush
at once; one that names nothing held is answered at once regardless.
.PP
The loop ends when the pipe ends, when a frame will not decode, or when
a
.B Tremove
is answered and
.I keep_on_remove
is
.BR false ,
which is its default.
.I Why
in
.IR serve 's
answer says which; the values are
.IR libuser.Serve_End ,
described in
.IR serve (2).
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Consrv (4)
starts the loop after posting, then unblocks every client it still owed
an answer to:
.IP
.EX
srv = lib9p.Srv{fd = fd, handler = handler, msize = FRAME}
libthread.threadcreate(key_thread, nil)
_, why := lib9p.serve(&srv)
lib9p.respond_all(&srv, vectra9.Rread{data = nil})
.EE
.PP
its handler holds a read that has nothing yet:
.IP
.EX
got := libuser.ring_drain(&ring, buf[:room])
if got > 0 {
	reply^ = vectra9.Rread{data = buf[:got]}
	return
}
lib9p.hold(&srv)
.EE
.PP
and its key thread answers held reads as bytes arrive:
.IP
.EX
lib9p.answer_reads(&srv, nil, wants_read, drain)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/lib9p/srv.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR serve (2),
.IR fid (2),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR flush (5),
.IR consrv (4),
.IR kbdfs (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Respond
answers
.B false
when the pipe refused the write, which the server treats as a hangup.
.SH BUGS
A request's record is one heap allocation, sized to
.IR msize ,
for every frame read; a server on a busy pipe pays that cost per
request.
